{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Our father died in the wilderness.</b> The reason they said <b>died in the wilderness</b> is to exclude that he did not die in Egypt, and the intention in this — both according to the one who says it was divided among those who came to the land and according to the one who says it was divided among those who left Egypt — this is the core of their claim. According to the one who says it was for those who left Egypt, they said 'died in the wilderness,' meaning he was one of those who left Egypt. And they also made a precise point in the word <b>in the wilderness</b> to say that he was included among those upon whom the decree of ״בַּמִּדְבָּר יִפְּלוּ פִגְרֵיכֶם״ (Numbers 14:29) was pronounced — from this you learn that he was twenty years old when he left Egypt. And according to the one who says the land was divided among those who came to it — he too concedes that it reverts to those who left Egypt and they bequeath it to their children, and the inheritance reaches them upon reversion." "<b>Further</b>, it may be explained according to the words of the Zohar (Zohar vol. II 157a) which states that the wilderness is the domain of the wicked Samael, and this is the reason for the immediate punishment of their sin: because they were treading upon him and subduing him, they could not attain this fence to exercise dominion over him until they were healed of the affliction of their sin. And this is what it says <b>died in the wilderness</b> — meaning, because he was in the wilderness, whereas otherwise HaShem would have extended His patience until he mended his deeds and he would not have died." "<b>Further</b>, it may be explained according to what they said in the Zohar (vol. III 200a) that ״בַּמִּדְבָּר״ is the language of speech [dibbur], for they came to remove from Moses' heart lest he suspect them — heaven forbid — that they harbored in their heart against him that he would pervert their judgment, and they said <b>our father died in the wilderness</b> — meaning: how could it enter the mind that they would think such a thing? Did they not see that HaShem killed their father because he spoke against Moses, and this is a sign for them to banish thoughts of evil against the man of Elohim. And they brought a proof for the matter, that their father's death was because he spoke against Moses — for he was not among the assembly, etc. And our Sages of blessed memory (Sifrei) expounded that they included in their words the assembly of the spies and the assembly of Korah and the complainers, and he was not among them. From this you learn that he had only the sin of speech, for several groups of wicked men existed and he was not counted among them. And this is what they concluded <b>for in his sin</b> — in the singular, meaning: he had one sin and no more. And perhaps it was for this that they intended to speak their words before all the princes and the assembly, to make these things heard in order to magnify the fear of Moses in their hearts. And it is possible that this was the intention in the verse's saying ״לֵאמֹר אָבִינוּ מֵת בַּמִּדְבָּר״ — meaning: the reason for ״וַתִּקְרַבְנָה לִפְנֵי״ etc. and ״וְלִפְנֵי״ etc. — to say this thing: our father died because he spoke against Moses, even though he was a complete righteous man and had only one sin." "<b>And with this</b> the difficulty of the Tosafot is resolved — they raised the question there in chapter Yesh Nochalin (Bava Batra 118a) in the passage beginning ״וּלְמַאן דְּאָמַר לְבָאֵי הָאָרֶץ״ etc. — for from the words of the Gemara it is proven that according to the one who says it was divided among those who came to the land, the daughters did not receive the portion of Zelophehad that pertained to him himself in the inheritance, but rather what pertained to him in the inheritance of Hepher when it reverted to his sons. If so, why did the daughters need to say ״וְהוּא לֹא הָיָה בְּתוֹךְ הָעֵדָה״ etc., for even the children of the assembly of the spies received by virtue of their father's father? According to our approach, they needed to say ״וְהוּא לֹא הָיָה״ etc., to say ״כִּי בְחֶטְאוֹ״ etc., as I have explained. And apart from our approach, the difficulty of the Tosafot can be resolved [by saying] that the daughters did not know that the spies were not absorbed to receive upon reversion in their father's property, and they thought that the inheritance in the land had been entirely forfeited by the spies — therefore they needed to say ״וְהוּא לֹא הָיָה״ etc." "<b>And he had no sons.</b> They did not say <b>he has none</b>; this may be explained according to the saying that children's children are regarded as children, and they raised in the Gemara (Yevamot 62b) that the same applies to a daughter's children. Therefore they said 'he did not have' — in the past, he did not — but there is hope that he will have, and these are the children whom his daughters will bear, who are also called his children:"], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:15:24.345068821Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}